## 2. Airflow metric → Dagster equivalent

The direct answer to "identify what the Dagster equivalent is." Airflow's documented metric set, mapped to what we would have to build.

**Legend for "How":**
- 🟢 **Free** — available from Kubernetes/infra with no Dagster changes
- 🟡 **Sensor** — derived from the Dagster event log via a run-status or polling sensor
- 🟠 **Code** — requires instrumentation inside pipeline code
- 🔴 **None** — no clean Dagster analogue

### 2.1 DAG run / job run level

| Airflow metric | Meaning | Dagster equivalent | How |
|---|---|---|---|
| `dagrun.duration.success.<dag_id>` | Successful run wall time | `dagster.run.duration{status="success"}` | 🟡 |
| `dagrun.duration.failed.<dag_id>` | Failed run wall time | `dagster.run.duration{status="failure"}` | 🟡 |
| `dagrun.schedule_delay.<dag_id>` | Scheduled → actual start gap | `dagster.run.schedule_delay` | 🟡 |
| `dagrun.first_task_scheduling_delay` | Run start → first task scheduled | `dagster.run.queue_duration` (enqueued → launched) | 🟡 |
| `dagrun.dependency-check.<dag_id>` | Dependency evaluation time | *None* — Dagster resolves the asset graph at definition time, not per run | 🔴 |
| `dag.<dag_id>.<task_id>.duration` | Task wall time | `dagster.op.duration{op_name}` | 🟠 |
| `ti.start.<dag_id>.<task_id>` | Task instance starts | `dagster.step.started` | 🟠 |
| `ti.finish.<dag_id>.<task_id>.<state>` | Task terminal state | `dagster.step.completed{status}` | 🟠 |
| `ti_successes` / `ti_failures` | Global task outcome counters | `dagster.step.completed{status}` aggregated | 🟠 |
| `operator_successes_<op>` / `operator_failures_<op>` | Outcome by operator type | `dagster.op.executions{op_name, status}` | 🟠 |
| `task_instance_created_<operator>` | Instances created by type | *Partial* — Dagster has no operator taxonomy; nearest is op/asset name | 🟠 |
| `previously_succeeded` | Re-run of an already-successful task | Only meaningful under memoization | 🔴 |

### 2.2 Scheduler / daemon level

| Airflow metric | Meaning | Dagster equivalent | How |
|---|---|---|---|
| `scheduler_heartbeat` | Scheduler alive | `dagster.daemon.heartbeat_age{daemon_type}` — daemons write heartbeats to the instance, readable via `instance.get_daemon_heartbeats()` | 🟡 |
| `scheduler.tasks.executable` | Tasks ready to run | `dagster.run.queued` | 🟡 |
| `scheduler.tasks.running` | Tasks currently running | `dagster.run.in_progress` | 🟡 |
| `scheduler.tasks.starving` | Tasks blocked on pool slots | `dagster.concurrency.blocked{key}` | 🟡 |
| `scheduler.tasks.killed_externally` | Killed outside Airflow | `kube_pod_container_status_last_terminated_reason` — genuinely better in Dagster's K8s model | 🟢 |
| `scheduler.critical_section_duration` | Scheduler loop lock time | *None* — the Dagster daemon has no equivalent critical section | 🔴 |
| `scheduler.orphaned_tasks.cleared` / `.adopted` | Orphan recovery | `dagster.run.monitoring_terminated` — the run-monitoring daemon marks stuck runs failed | 🟡 |
| `zombies_killed` | Zombie cleanup | Same as above | 🟡 |

### 2.3 Executor / pool level

| Airflow metric | Meaning | Dagster equivalent | How |
|---|---|---|---|
| `executor.open_slots` | Free executor capacity | `dagster.run_queue.available_slots` — `max_concurrent_runs` minus in-progress | 🟡 |
| `executor.queued_tasks` | Queued in executor | `dagster.run.queued` | 🟡 |
| `executor.running_tasks` | Running in executor | `dagster.run.in_progress` | 🟡 |
| `pool.open_slots.<pool>` | Free slots in a named pool | `dagster.concurrency.available{key}` | 🟡 |
| `pool.queued_slots.<pool>` | Queued against a pool | `dagster.concurrency.pending{key}` | 🟡 |
| `pool.starving_tasks.<pool>` | Starved by pool exhaustion | `dagster.concurrency.blocked{key}` | 🟡 |

> **Conceptual caveat:** Airflow pools and Dagster concurrency keys are similar but not identical. Airflow pools are statically declared with a fixed slot count; Dagster's concurrency keys are tag-based limits enforced by the run coordinator and instance. The mapping is directional, not exact.

### 2.4 DAG parsing / code loading

| Airflow metric | Meaning | Dagster equivalent | How |
|---|---|---|---|
| `dagbag_size` | DAGs loaded | `dagster.definitions.count{code_location, type}` | 🟡 |
| `dag_processing.import_errors` | Files failing to parse | `dagster.code_location.healthy{location}` | 🟡 |
| `dag_processing.total_parse_time` | Total parse time | `dagster.code_location.load_duration` — gRPC definitions-load time | 🟠 |
| `dag_processing.last_duration.<file>` | Per-file parse time | Per-code-location load time (coarser — Dagster's unit is the location, not the file) | 🟠 |
| `dag_processing.last_run.seconds_ago.<file>` | Parse staleness | *None* — Dagster code servers load once at startup, not on a reparse loop | 🔴 |
| `dag_processing.processor_timeouts` | Parse timeouts | `dagster.code_location.load_timeout` | 🟡 |
| `dag_file_refresh_error` | Refresh failures | Code-location reload failures | 🟡 |

### 2.5 What Dagster has that Airflow does not

Worth stating in the ticket — the mapping is not purely a deficit:

| Dagster-native metric | Why Airflow has no equivalent |
|---|---|
| `dagster.asset.materialization{asset_key, status}` | No first-class asset concept in the same sense |
| `dagster.asset.materialization_lag{asset_key}` | Freshness relative to policy is a native primitive |
| `dagster.asset.check.result{asset_key, check_name, status}` | Data-quality checks are a native primitive |
| `dagster.asset.rows_written{asset_key}` | Materialization metadata carries arbitrary business values |
| `dagster.backfill.progress{backfill_id}` | Partitioned backfills are a native primitive |
| `dagster.sensor.tick{sensor_name, status}` | Sensors are richer than Airflow sensor operators |

