## 3. The Dagster metric catalog

The concrete answer to *"if no equivalent exists, what Dagster-specific metrics do we need to send to Otel."*

### 3.1 Tier 1 — operational health (build first)

| Metric | Type | Unit | Attributes | Why |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `dagster.run.completed` | Counter | — | `job_name`, `status`, `code_location` | Success rate — the core SLI |
| `dagster.run.duration` | Histogram | s | `job_name`, `code_location` | Latency SLO, regression detection |
| `dagster.run.queue_duration` | Histogram | s | `job_name` | Capacity signal — rising means under-provisioned |
| `dagster.run.in_progress` | UpDownCounter | — | `code_location` | Live concurrency |
| `dagster.run.queued` | Gauge | — | `code_location` | Backlog depth |
| `dagster.daemon.heartbeat_age` | Gauge | s | `daemon_type` | **Highest-priority alert** |
| `dagster.code_location.healthy` | Gauge | — | `location` | 0/1 — a failed location silently stops all its schedules |

### 3.2 Tier 2 — pipeline detail

| Metric | Type | Unit | Attributes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `dagster.op.duration` | Histogram | s | `job_name`, `op_name` |
| `dagster.op.executions` | Counter | — | `job_name`, `op_name`, `status` |
| `dagster.step.retries` | Counter | — | `job_name`, `op_name` |
| `dagster.sensor.tick` | Counter | — | `sensor_name`, `status` |
| `dagster.sensor.tick_duration` | Histogram | s | `sensor_name` |
| `dagster.schedule.tick` | Counter | — | `schedule_name`, `status` |

### 3.3 Tier 3 — data / asset semantics

| Metric | Type | Unit | Attributes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `dagster.asset.materialization` | Counter | — | `asset_key`, `status` |
| `dagster.asset.materialization_lag` | Gauge | s | `asset_key` |
| `dagster.asset.check.result` | Counter | — | `asset_key`, `check_name`, `status` |
| `dagster.asset.rows_written` | Histogram | — | `asset_key` |
| `dagster.backfill.progress` | Gauge | 1 | `backfill_id`, `job_name` |

### 3.4 Cardinality rules — read before implementing

**Never put these on a metric:** `run_id`, `step_key`, `partition_key`, any timestamp, any file path, any user-supplied free text.

`run_id` is a UUID. At 500 runs/day, one counter carrying a `run_id` attribute creates ~182,500 new time series per year, permanently. This is the single most common way teams destroy a Prometheus/Mimir install.

`run_id` belongs on **spans and log records**, where high cardinality is free. Enforce this at the Collector (§5.2) as a safety net rather than trusting every future contributor to remember.

`asset_key`, `job_name`, `op_name`, and `sensor_name` are acceptable because their cardinality is bounded by the codebase, not by execution volume.

